okay there is a regular and constant  argument against increases to the  minimum wage typically from like the  supply-side economics types for the  people that at least think they  understand what that is if you pay more  it hurts employment you reduce the  number of jobs and eventually the total  demand for labor actually goes down and  you hurt the economy this is an argument  that doesn't make sense for a number of  reasons number one it leaves no room for  other variations in the labor force and  number two we've also seen anecdotally  that this doesn't actually happen we've  been covering what's been going on in  san francisco county california for  years now where they were way ahead of  the rest of the country in raising the  minimum wage they're above and beyond uh  even what what many states currently  have is the minimum wage they were years  ahead of it this applied also to fast  food which is one of those industries  that's often sort of considered to be as  soon as you raise minimum wage it really  hurts fast-food employees and fast food  restaurants no negative repercussions  now you could still say well okay David  fine it's not true that necessarily you  hurt jobs by raising the minimum wage  and it's not true that necessarily  raising the minimum wage hurts fast food  but that's just San Francisco San  Francisco is a very high income area  it's a very unique situation where it's  a high cost of living but also very very  wealthy area you need to look elsewhere  well fortunately for us that's exactly  what the Center for Economic and Policy  Research did they analyzed a report by  the White House Council of Economic  Advisers on the effects of raising the  minimum wage beyond the federal minimum  okay so they say okay here is where we  are based on the federal minimum wage of  725 let's see what impact unemployment  in the economy going beyond that federal  minimum has the researchers found that  the 18 states plus Washington DC that  increased wages beyond the federal  minimum wage in 2014 number one it led  to low income workers benefiting that  obviously makes sense  but it also saw no uh-uh depression of  jobs available to low-income workers  there was no additional economic harm uh  found to those increases above and  beyond the federal minimum wage this  actually increased average wages in  low-wage jobs but it also reversed a  trend generally of falling real wages  that we saw and we did not see a  negative impact on jobs the CEA for  those who say hey it's a white House  Counsel the CEA doesn't have its own  data they use Bureau of Labor Statistics  data from for workers and leisure and  hospitality and I've talked many times  before if you don't believe the Bureau  of Labor Statistics data under President  Obama then you actually can't really  argue that unemployment is high or low  because it's all being compared to  Bureau of Labor Statistics data under  other presidents and it's very hard to  argue that only under President Obama is  that data not trustworthy so those who  argue against for example safety net  programs or raising the minimum wage or  having a basic income they often go  beyond the economic argument many of  them say the problem David is not the  economic argument fine maybe these  programs raising the minimum wage  welfare programs it's not bad for the  economy but we can't just be handing  things out to people and this gets us  back to this concept which George Lake  off talked about quite a bit of strict  father morality which is when as  progressives we identify a Republican  position we analyze it and we say hey  you know what it turns out that even  though Republicans say they're concerned  with fiscal conservatism they support a  policy that is not fiscally conservative  or it's not pro-business layer two is  fundamentally strict father morality and  the idea of people only having what they  quote deserve according to conservatives  will actually override the supposedly  important principle of fiscal  conservative conservatism and making  good economic decisions last thing on  this  uh anytime we talk about raising the  minimum wage some people will write into  me and I'll say David if raising the  minimum wage is good just raise it to  200 bucks an hour then everybody will  have tons of money do not fall for that  red herring that red herring ignores  that no one is calling for raising the  minimum wage to two hundred dollars an  hour when we talk about twelve fifteen  or eighteen dollars an hour we're  talking about minimum wages in a range  that can be justified by changes to cost  of living and increases in productivity  and what it takes to earn a living wage  in different parts of the United States  that is what is called uh reductio ad  absurdum when you say well if it's good  to raise it from twelve to thirteen  raising it to 200 must be really good no  no one is arguing that that is what  should be done no serious economist are  saying that you have to base the minimum  wage on the economic realities of a  living wage and what would be an  equilibrium wage in our economy uh the  two hundred dollar minimum wage red  herring you have to be ready to debunk  that because it makes absolutely no  sense whatsoever let's take a break  still plenty more coming up glad you're  with me on this Thursday thanks for  watching this video before you watch the  next one remember we're raising money to  expand the david pakman show big league  as well as expand progressive activism  go to david pakman.com slash donate  donations of any size are much  appreciated
 

